#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268903 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124557 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2d7d4db-e9e1-54a0-aadb-42447771855d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain Where All Partial Derivatives Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01bb0d90-7259-5d35-8fde-f279ac853c2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1,\\,z\\ne 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266159} {:answer/id #uuid "0d271da6-7c48-5d4f-aa3a-28d0e699fc21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 1,\\,z\\ne 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266263} {:answer/id #uuid "92048fda-387f-5596-a311-1b73f7391467" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0,\\,z>0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267444} {:answer/id #uuid "b73271e7-a607-5729-8cd2-22d3cf892f47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267786} {:answer/id #uuid "f1e3d159-8824-5ef2-96dc-eee22976133e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268294}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01bb0d90-7259-5d35-8fde-f279ac853c2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1,\\,z\\ne 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266159} :answer-field/id #uuid "544bcd11-f607-53b0-99d1-1273ed387e89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268559}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f03ff15b-864a-54f0-95c4-85eaad4622ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-132650" :question/problem "Given that $g(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)}{z}$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂g}{∂x}=\\frac{2x}{z({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)},\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=\\frac{2y}{z({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)},\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂z}=-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)}{{z}^{2}},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y,z)$ where all partial derivatives of $g$ exist." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $g(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)}{z}$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1>0$ and $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of $g$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{g}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1,\\,z\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${g}_{x}(x,y,z)=\\frac{2x}{z({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)}$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1\\ne 0$ and $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{x}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 1,\\,z\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domain of ${g}_{y}(x,y,z)$ is the same: \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{y}}={D}_{{g}_{x}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 1,\\,z\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${g}_{z}(x,y,z)=-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)}{{z}^{2}}$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1>0$ and $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{z}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{z}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1,\\,z\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $g$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{g}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{z}} & ={D}_{g}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{z}} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1,\\,z\\ne 0} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 1,\\,z\\ne 0} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1,\\,z\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1,\\,z\\ne 0}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426720 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124557 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2d7d4db-e9e1-54a0-aadb-42447771855d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain Where All Partial Derivatives Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a414987-4111-5550-9b84-35b48e518248" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0,\\,z\\ne n\\pi \\},\\,n\\in Z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426709} {:answer/id #uuid "c4574d37-c2e2-5922-8956-3143f5bef26f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y>0,\\,z\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426712} {:answer/id #uuid "c67ec9fa-9f76-59b9-b6ed-a85557705824" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y>0,\\,z\\ne n\\pi \\},\\,n\\in Z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426713} {:answer/id #uuid "ee7e5c9b-05ac-5232-9af8-bc55f1d66335" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0,\\,z\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426715} {:answer/id #uuid "f480f4b0-b440-57d3-be41-894b18986911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0,\\,z\\ne n\\pi \\},\\,n\\in Z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f480f4b0-b440-57d3-be41-894b18986911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0,\\,z\\ne n\\pi \\},\\,n\\in Z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426716} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d0f4a07-8c58-5c53-b0aa-71dedf0ab601" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426719}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "13585dd1-9ab5-5887-870c-2d899c40d4a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-132652" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{x}{y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡z},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}{y{sin}^{2}⁡z}\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y,z)$ where all partial derivatives of $f$ exist." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{x}{y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}$ is defined only when $y\\ne 0$ and $z\\ne n\\pi $ where $n\\in Z.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0,z\\ne n\\pi \\},\\,n\\in Z.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${f}_{x}(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}$ is defined only when $y\\ne 0$ and $z\\ne n\\pi $ where $n\\in Z.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0,z\\ne n\\pi \\},\\,n\\in Z.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domains of ${f}_{y}$ and ${f}_{z}$ are the same:\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={D}_{{f}_{z}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0,z\\ne n\\pi \\},\\,n\\in Z.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & ={D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0,z\\ne n\\pi \\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0,z\\ne n\\pi \\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0,z\\ne n\\pi \\},\\,n\\in Z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268906 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124466 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d090462-a031-5f96-ac13-90dac882edab" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points Where Partial Derivatives Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e430633-0c79-5bd0-864a-cc54142526aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449496} {:answer/id #uuid "99da71ad-b733-50e9-9bc1-232efc6cef4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99da71ad-b733-50e9-9bc1-232efc6cef4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449497} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad351a32-8420-5f40-bd4e-3a76a453aa9b" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450702} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e1d2654-71e6-5045-abb7-876f75070a78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449498} {:answer/id #uuid "9a1659ae-9585-5275-84c0-eb9540469367" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449499}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e1d2654-71e6-5045-abb7-876f75070a78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449498} :answer-field/id #uuid "f83c3b78-80d6-5799-ba6a-86046c4eb7a9" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450703} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b65c5da8-b876-59b5-b16a-4a0a833c5303" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449500} {:answer/id #uuid "c93dd213-5575-5cca-b228-64873eafdfe4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449501}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b65c5da8-b876-59b5-b16a-4a0a833c5303" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449500} :answer-field/id #uuid "db1f014c-1d1e-5df1-9579-1cb65d8ea1ac" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450704}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "72f1f14f-947a-5dd6-902d-418c94ea31fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-132675" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y,z)=z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The partial derivative with respect to $y$ is defined at $(-1,-1,0)$\n- **II.** ${f}_{x}(0,1,1)$ is defined\n- **III.** ${f}_{z}(1,-1,1)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy) \\\\  & =z\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =\\frac{z}{x} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy) \\\\  & =z\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =\\frac{z}{y} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂z}(z) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(-1,-1,0)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(-1,-1,0)=0\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡((-1)\\cdot (-1))=0,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(-1,-1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-1,-1,0) & =\\frac{0}{(-1)}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(0,1,1)$ into ${f}_{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(0,1,1)=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\n- Statement III is also false. If we substitute $(1,-1,1)$ into ${f}_{z}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{z}(1,-1,1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1\\cdot (-1))=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-1),\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is defined only for $x>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124995 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124466 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d090462-a031-5f96-ac13-90dac882edab" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points Where Partial Derivatives Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b903ce0b-98c6-57d8-9e48-735e14bb633f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448584} {:answer/id #uuid "e89b1128-0cdb-5f83-9979-822275c3412d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e89b1128-0cdb-5f83-9979-822275c3412d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448585} :answer-field/id #uuid "62e75cd6-e230-5ef5-921b-7e8382a039af" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449332} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b15a164-a197-53c5-b168-a0a97d0c6836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448586} {:answer/id #uuid "c7106db2-aab9-5e51-b215-01c8b91bedc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448587}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b15a164-a197-53c5-b168-a0a97d0c6836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448586} :answer-field/id #uuid "028cafa1-ca77-5fce-9098-ff08007d68ae" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449333} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0b2a1b1-098a-5a44-83b4-193b393fe615" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448588} {:answer/id #uuid "5b241cba-015b-566d-8a54-d752edb0b3b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0b2a1b1-098a-5a44-83b4-193b393fe615" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448588} :answer-field/id #uuid "17e7993b-564d-5df7-9cb5-369c37279a0f" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449334}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cee8f940-b17d-518d-bf59-666639ac6929" :question/math-academy-id "q-36504" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{x+y})?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(1,1)$\n- **II.** ${f}_{x}(0,1)$ is defined\n- **III.** ${f}_{y}(0,1)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $f$ is defined only when\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{x+y}>0.\n$$\n\nAt points in the domain of $f,$ we can compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{x+y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x+y}{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{x+y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x+y}{x}\\cdot \\frac{1\\cdot (x+y)-x\\cdot 1}{(x+y)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+y}{x}\\cdot \\frac{y}{(x+y)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x(x+y)} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{x+y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x+y}{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{x+y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x+y}{x}\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{(x+y)^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x+y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is defined at $(1,1),$ since\n\n$$\nf(1,1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{1+1})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}),\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are defined there:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,1) & =\\frac{1}{1(1+1)}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,1) & =-\\frac{1}{1+1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. At $(0,1),$\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{x+y}=\\frac{0}{0+1}=0,\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\nf(0,1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0),\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined. Therefore, ${f}_{x}(0,1)$ is not defined.\n\n- Statement III is false for the same reason. Since $f$ is not defined at $(0,1),$ the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(0,1)$ is not defined. The simplified expression $-\\frac{1}{x+y}$ cannot be used to define the partial derivative at a point outside the domain of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}]}
